(() => {
'use strict';
const app=window.EnglishActivities;
if(!app)return;
const esc=value=>String(value??'').replace(/[&<>"']/g,char=>({'&':'&amp;','<':'&lt;','>':'&gt;','"':'&quot;',"'":'&#39;'}[char]));
const courseNames={philosophy:'Philosophy',history:'History',startups:'Startups',economy:'Economy',cinema:'Cinema',literature:'Literature'};

const newReadings={
 philosophy:[
  {code:'F14',title:'Ethics: Virtue, Duty, and Consequences',summary:'Three major traditions for judging action and building a life worth living.',objective:'Compare virtue ethics, deontology, and consequentialism without reducing moral judgment to a single formula.',sections:[
   ['The question behind ethical theory',['Ethics asks more than whether an isolated act is permitted. It asks what kind of person one should become, which obligations people owe one another, and which institutions make good lives possible. Moral conflicts become difficult when values that normally support one another—care, honesty, loyalty, freedom, and justice—pull in different directions.','A theory is useful when it clarifies what matters, exposes hidden assumptions, and helps people give reasons that others can examine. It becomes dangerous when it is treated as an automatic machine that removes responsibility from the person making the decision.']],
   ['Aristotle and the formation of character',['Aristotle begins with flourishing rather than a list of commands. Courage, generosity, honesty, and practical wisdom become stable through repeated action. A virtue is not a passive feeling; it is a trained capacity to perceive what a situation requires and to respond well.','The doctrine of the mean does not recommend mediocrity. Courage is a mean between cowardice and recklessness relative to a person and a circumstance. Practical wisdom is necessary because no rule can specify in advance the right response to every concrete case.']],
   ['Kant and respect for persons',['Kant asks whether the principle of an action could be willed as a universal law. If a promise works only because most people keep promises, a universal practice of false promising would destroy the institution on which the deception depends.','His second formulation insists that persons must be treated as ends and never merely as tools. This does not forbid cooperation or exchange; it forbids arrangements that bypass another person’s capacity to choose through coercion, deception, or exploitation.']],
   ['Mill, consequences, and public welfare',['Utilitarian traditions evaluate actions and rules by their effects on well-being. They force moral reasoning to include every affected person and to take preventable suffering seriously. This is especially powerful in public health, policy, and decisions involving scarce resources.','The difficulty is measurement. Benefits and harms are distributed unevenly, future effects are uncertain, and a majority can gain at a minority’s expense. Contemporary consequentialists therefore debate rights, rules, capabilities, and the quality—not only the quantity—of human goods.']],
   ['A case: truth during a crisis',['Imagine a public institution that discovers a serious but containable risk. Immediate disclosure may create panic; delay may prevent confusion but also deny people the ability to protect themselves. Virtue ethics asks what honesty and practical wisdom demand. Kantian ethics examines respect and the permissibility of withholding information. Consequentialism compares likely harms under alternative communication strategies.','The case shows why mature judgment can use several lenses at once. The goal is not to select the theory that excuses a preferred answer, but to discover which moral cost each option carries and which reasons can be defended publicly.']]
  ],questions:['Which virtues are necessary for responsible public leadership?','When does protecting someone become a form of disrespect?','Can a good consequence justify a deceptive method?','Which moral costs remain even after the best available decision?'],terms:[['virtue ethics','ethics centered on character and flourishing'],['deontology','ethics centered on duties and principles'],['consequentialism','ethics centered on the effects of actions or rules'],['practical wisdom','the capacity to judge well in concrete situations']]},
  {code:'F15',title:'Political Philosophy: Authority, Freedom, and Justice',summary:'Hobbes, Locke, Rousseau, Rawls, and the problem of legitimate power.',objective:'Distinguish political order from legitimate authority and compare influential accounts of freedom, rights, equality, and consent.',sections:[
   ['Why political power needs justification',['Every political order uses rules backed by institutions and, ultimately, coercive force. Political philosophy asks when that force is legitimate. Stability alone is insufficient: a regime may be stable because dissent is impossible. Consent alone is also complicated, because most people are born into institutions they never explicitly chose.','The central problem is how people who disagree about religion, morality, property, and the good life can share laws without becoming subjects of arbitrary power. Different traditions answer by emphasizing security, natural rights, popular sovereignty, democratic participation, or fair cooperation.']],
   ['Hobbes and the priority of security',['Hobbes imagines a condition without a common authority, where insecurity makes durable cooperation fragile. Individuals authorize a sovereign because peace is a precondition for industry, knowledge, and ordinary life. Political obligation grows from the need to escape mutual vulnerability.','His solution raises a lasting objection: if the sovereign must be strong enough to secure order, how can subjects be protected from the sovereign itself? Hobbes makes security visible as a political achievement, but he leaves limited institutional space for resisting abuse.']],
   ['Locke and rights against government',['Locke grounds political society in rights to life, liberty, and property. Government is a fiduciary power: it receives authority for public purposes and loses legitimacy when it systematically violates the rights it was created to protect.','This tradition supports constitutional limits and a right of resistance. It also carries historical tensions. The language of universal rights developed alongside colonial expansion, dispossession, and unequal definitions of who could count as an independent political subject.']],
   ['Rousseau, freedom, and participation',['Rousseau argues that dependence on another person’s private will is a form of unfreedom. Citizens are free when they participate as equals in making general laws rather than merely obeying a ruler or protecting private interests.','The general will is not whatever a majority happens to want. It names a demanding orientation toward common freedom. Yet its language can be abused when leaders claim to embody the people and silence actual disagreement. Democratic participation therefore requires institutions that preserve plurality and accountability.']],
   ['Rawls and fair cooperation',['Rawls asks which principles people would choose behind a veil of ignorance, not knowing their class, talents, religion, gender, or social position. The thought experiment models impartiality by preventing negotiators from designing rules around their private advantages.','His principles protect equal basic liberties and permit inequalities only under fair opportunity and when they benefit the least advantaged. Critics ask whether the model is too idealized, whether it treats historical injustice adequately, and whether care, disability, race, colonialism, and global inequality require different starting points.']]
  ],questions:['Is security a condition of freedom or a possible excuse for suppressing it?','What would genuine political consent require?','Can majority rule coexist with domination?','Which inequalities could no one reasonably reject?'],terms:[['legitimacy','the justified right to exercise political authority'],['social contract','a model that explains political order through agreement'],['popular sovereignty','the principle that political power comes from the people'],['veil of ignorance','Rawls’s device for reasoning impartially about justice']]},
  {code:'F16',title:'Mind, Language, and Personal Identity',summary:'Consciousness, meaning, memory, embodiment, and the question of what makes a person the same over time.',objective:'Connect classic problems of mind and language with contemporary debates about identity, artificial intelligence, and embodied experience.',sections:[
   ['The mind-body problem',['Mental life seems both intimate and physical. Pain has a felt quality, yet it is related to nerves, brains, behavior, language, and social interpretation. Dualism emphasizes that subjective experience cannot simply be described as matter in motion. Physicalism argues that mental events depend on physical systems and should ultimately be explained within nature.','The debate is not solved by naming the brain. A complete account must explain how first-person experience, reasons, intentions, and meaning relate to third-person observation. The challenge is to avoid turning consciousness into a mysterious substance or eliminating the phenomenon that requires explanation.']],
   ['Meaning is not a private object',['Words do not carry meaning like containers. Their meaning depends on patterns of use, contrast, context, and shared practices. Wittgenstein’s idea of language games shifts attention from an invisible definition inside the mind to what speakers do with words in forms of life.','This does not make meaning arbitrary. Practices have standards, histories, and possibilities for correction. It does show why the same sentence can function as a promise, threat, joke, request, or quotation depending on circumstances and relationships.']],
   ['Memory and continuity',['One influential account connects personal identity with psychological continuity: memories, intentions, character, and projects. Yet memory can fail, be reconstructed, or depend on records and other people. Bodily continuity offers another criterion, but even the body changes and can be technologically supported.','Narrative theories propose that identity is not a hidden core but an ongoing interpretation that organizes change. A narrative can give continuity without being fiction in the sense of falsehood. It remains answerable to evidence, relationships, and the consequences of how a person describes a life.']],
   ['Embodiment and other minds',['Phenomenology emphasizes that consciousness is embodied and situated. We do not first receive neutral data and then infer a world; we encounter meaningful possibilities through perception, movement, habit, and concern. Other people are likewise encountered through expression and shared activity, not as theoretical puzzles alone.','This perspective changes how disability, emotion, technology, and social space are studied. A tool can become integrated into action, while an environment can expand or restrict what a body is able to do.']],
   ['Artificial intelligence as a philosophical test',['A language model can produce fluent answers without sharing a human biography, body, or social vulnerability. Does successful performance demonstrate understanding, simulate it, or force us to revise what understanding means? The answer depends on whether intelligence is defined by behavior, internal representation, embodied participation, or responsible membership in a community.','The strongest inquiry avoids two shortcuts: assuming that human-like language proves human-like consciousness, and assuming that unfamiliar architecture makes genuine cognition impossible. Philosophy clarifies the criteria being used and the evidence each criterion would require.']]
  ],questions:['Could a perfect physical description include what pain feels like?','Can a language understood by only one person have rules?','Which changes can a person survive while remaining the same person?','What evidence would count for or against machine understanding?'],terms:[['dualism','the view that mind and matter are fundamentally distinct'],['physicalism','the view that mental reality depends on physical reality'],['language game','a rule-governed practice in which words have use'],['psychological continuity','connections of memory, intention, and character over time']]}
 ],
 history:[
  {code:'H17',title:'Ancient China and the Silk Roads',summary:'State formation, philosophical traditions, exchange networks, and the movement of goods and ideas across Eurasia.',objective:'Understand early Chinese political development and the Silk Roads as changing networks rather than a single continuous route.',sections:[
   ['Dynasties and political legitimacy',['The Zhou developed the idea of the Mandate of Heaven: rulers retained authority only while preserving order and moral legitimacy. Because disaster and rebellion could be interpreted as signs that the mandate had been lost, dynastic change acquired a political language that combined cosmic order with human responsibility.','During the Warring States period, competition produced military innovation, administrative reform, and intense philosophical debate. The Qin unified territories through standardization and centralized power, while the Han preserved imperial unity through a more durable combination of bureaucracy, law, ritual, and local elites.']],
   ['Confucian, Daoist, and Legalist responses',['Confucian traditions emphasized cultivated character, ritual relationships, learning, and government by exemplary conduct. Daoist texts questioned rigid distinctions and celebrated responsiveness to changing conditions. Legalist thinkers concentrated on institutions, incentives, clear standards, and the ruler’s control of officials.','These traditions were never isolated boxes. Imperial governments could teach Confucian ideals, use Legalist administrative techniques, and draw on cosmological ideas associated with several schools. Intellectual history becomes clearer when doctrines are studied as arguments within political problems.']],
   ['What the Silk Roads connected',['The Silk Roads were overlapping land and maritime routes linking East Asia, Central Asia, South Asia, the Middle East, Africa, and Europe. Silk was important, but traders also moved horses, glass, spices, metals, paper, artistic forms, and financial techniques.','Most merchants traveled only part of the distance. Goods changed hands through cities, caravan networks, ports, and political frontiers. Empires could protect trade, tax it, redirect it, or make routes dangerous through warfare.']],
   ['Religions, technologies, and disease',['Buddhism traveled into China through translators, monasteries, pilgrims, and patrons. Concepts changed as texts entered new languages and interacted with existing traditions. Paper-making and other techniques also moved through complex processes of adaptation rather than simple one-way transfer.','Connections carried danger as well as creativity. Epidemics followed routes of war and exchange. The same networks that widened markets and intellectual horizons could transmit disease across regions with different levels of immunity.']],
   ['Reading a connected history',['A connected history does not erase local difference. It asks how an event in one region changed the possibilities available elsewhere. Chinese demand affected Central Asian politics; Roman and Persian markets influenced long-distance exchange; nomadic confederations controlled strategic corridors.','The result is not a story of inevitable globalization. Networks expanded, contracted, and shifted toward maritime routes according to technology, ecology, state power, and conflict.']]
  ],questions:['How did political ideas justify both obedience and rebellion?','Why is the image of one Silk Road misleading?','What changes when a religion is translated into a new culture?','How can exchange create prosperity and vulnerability at the same time?'],terms:[['Mandate of Heaven','a doctrine linking rule to moral and cosmic legitimacy'],['bureaucracy','administration through appointed officials and procedures'],['caravan','a group traveling together for trade and security'],['cultural transmission','the movement and transformation of ideas or practices']]},
  {code:'H18',title:'African Kingdoms, Cities, and Knowledge Before Colonial Rule',summary:'Political diversity, trade, learning, and historical evidence across precolonial Africa.',objective:'Replace the myth of a continent without history with a comparative view of African states, communities, and knowledge systems.',sections:[
   ['A continent of many historical paths',['Africa contains extraordinary ecological and linguistic diversity. Pastoral societies, farming communities, forest states, trading cities, and mobile confederations developed different political forms. No single sequence from tribe to kingdom to empire can describe them all.','Historians combine archaeology, oral traditions, linguistics, art, environmental evidence, and written sources in Arabic, Ge’ez, Portuguese, and other languages. Each source reveals certain relationships and hides others, so comparison is essential.']],
   ['West African empires and trans-Saharan trade',['Ghana, Mali, and Songhai became powerful by controlling territory, taxation, and exchange across the Sahel. Gold moved north while salt, textiles, books, and other goods moved through caravan networks. Rulers negotiated relationships with merchants, scholars, farmers, and pastoral groups.','Mansa Musa’s pilgrimage displayed Mali’s wealth but should not reduce the empire to one famous journey. Its power depended on administration, agriculture, military organization, and cities connected to wider commercial and intellectual worlds.']],
   ['Timbuktu and cultures of scholarship',['Timbuktu and other centers supported mosques, schools, libraries, copyists, and legal scholarship. Manuscripts addressed theology, law, astronomy, medicine, grammar, and commerce. Learning was embedded in families, patronage, and travel across North and West Africa.','Written scholarship existed alongside oral forms of historical memory. Griots preserved genealogies, political agreements, praise, and criticism through skilled performance. Oral tradition is not an unchanged recording; like every archive, it must be studied in relation to audience and power.']],
   ['Indian Ocean cities and southern states',['Swahili cities connected the East African coast to Arabia, Persia, India, and later China through maritime trade. Their architecture, language, and material culture reveal local African foundations shaped by long contact.','Farther south, Great Zimbabwe grew through cattle, agriculture, craft production, and trade in gold and ivory. Colonial interpreters once denied that Africans could have built it, showing how racism can distort archaeological explanation.']],
   ['History against colonial categories',['European colonial rule later classified mobile identities into rigid ethnic categories and presented conquest as the arrival of civilization. A longer history shows sophisticated institutions as well as conflict, inequality, slavery, and political change before European domination.','Correcting colonial myths does not require romanticizing the past. It requires applying the same historical standards used elsewhere: evidence, chronology, comparison, and attention to both achievement and violence.']]
  ],questions:['Why must historians combine different kinds of evidence?','How did trade influence state power without determining it completely?','What can oral traditions preserve that state archives may omit?','How did colonial assumptions shape interpretations of African monuments?'],terms:[['Sahel','the semi-arid region south of the Sahara'],['griot','a specialist in oral history and performance'],['Swahili city-state','an urban trading polity on the East African coast'],['oral tradition','knowledge transmitted through spoken performance across generations']]},
  {code:'H19',title:'Atlantic Revolutions, Slavery, and Abolition',summary:'Liberty, empire, racial slavery, and revolutionary transformation from the late eighteenth to the nineteenth century.',objective:'Compare the American, French, Haitian, and Spanish American revolutions through their promises, exclusions, and Atlantic connections.',sections:[
   ['An age of connected revolutions',['Wars, fiscal crises, print culture, imperial reform, and Enlightenment arguments destabilized Atlantic empires. Revolutionaries used languages of natural rights, citizenship, sovereignty, and representation, but these concepts acquired different meanings in different conflicts.','News, people, and political texts crossed borders. Later movements learned from earlier successes and failures, while authorities also learned how to suppress rebellion. The Atlantic was a field of exchange structured by war and slavery.']],
   ['Independence and exclusion in North America',['The American Revolution challenged parliamentary taxation and imperial authority. Its constitutions expanded republican government, yet the new order protected slavery and displaced Indigenous nations. Women participated in boycotts, production, political discussion, and wartime labor without receiving equal citizenship.','This contradiction was not a minor delay outside the revolution. Enslaved people pursued freedom through escape, military service, petitions, and their own interpretations of revolutionary language.']],
   ['France and the remaking of political order',['The French Revolution abolished feudal privileges, declared rights, reorganized institutions, and mobilized citizens on an unprecedented scale. It also moved through monarchy, republic, terror, war, and empire.','Universal claims collided with property, gender, religion, colonial interests, and political emergency. Olympe de Gouges and other critics exposed the gap between the abstract citizen and the people excluded from full political membership.']],
   ['Haiti and the limits of universalism',['The Haitian Revolution began in the richest slave colony in the Caribbean. Enslaved people destroyed slavery through organized revolt, military struggle, and political negotiation, defeating French, Spanish, and British forces.','Haitian independence made universal freedom more concrete than declarations that left colonial slavery intact. Slaveholding societies responded with fear, isolation, and economic punishment, while Black activists across the Atlantic drew inspiration from the revolution.']],
   ['Spanish American independence and abolition',['The collapse of Spanish authority during the Napoleonic invasion opened struggles over sovereignty. Creole elites, Indigenous communities, enslaved and free Black people, urban groups, and regional forces pursued different aims. Independence produced new republics without automatically ending social hierarchy.','Abolition was usually gradual and contested. Military recruitment, free-womb laws, resistance by enslaved people, international pressure, and economic change contributed to slavery’s destruction, but racial inequality survived in labor systems, property, and citizenship.']]
  ],questions:['Why did universal rights coexist with slavery?','What makes Haiti central rather than peripheral to revolutionary history?','Which groups turned elite conflicts into broader social revolutions?','What survived after legal slavery ended?'],terms:[['abolition','the legal and political destruction of slavery'],['sovereignty','supreme authority within a political community'],['emancipation','the process of gaining freedom from slavery'],['Atlantic world','the connected societies around the Atlantic Ocean']]}
 ],
 startups:[
  {code:'S22',title:'Business Models and Unit Economics',summary:'How value creation, pricing, costs, and customer behavior combine into a sustainable business.',objective:'Translate a product idea into an explicit model of who receives value, who pays, and how each transaction affects the company.',sections:[
   ['A product is not yet a business model',['A product can delight users and still fail as a business. A business model explains the customer, the job being done, the value proposition, the channel, the revenue mechanism, the resources required, and the costs created by delivery.','The model should fit on one page at first because its purpose is to expose assumptions. Detail is added when evidence makes it useful, not to make uncertainty look precise.']],
   ['Revenue and pricing architecture',['Subscription, transaction, usage, licensing, advertising, and marketplace fees produce different incentives. A subscription rewards retention; a transaction model depends on frequency and trust; usage pricing aligns payment with consumption but can make costs unpredictable.','Pricing is part of product design. Tiers, limits, bundles, and contract terms shape which customers adopt, how they use the product, and what the company must support.']],
   ['Unit economics',['Contribution margin measures what remains from a unit of revenue after variable costs. Customer acquisition cost estimates the resources required to gain a customer, while lifetime value estimates the gross contribution expected during the relationship.','A ratio is not a law. Lifetime value becomes fictional when retention history is short, customers differ radically, or future margins are assumed rather than observed. Cohort analysis is safer than a single company-wide average.']],
   ['Growth can hide a broken engine',['A company can increase revenue while losing more money on every customer. Discounts may accelerate acquisition but attract low-intent users. A long payback period may be acceptable for durable enterprise contracts and fatal for a startup with little cash.','Founders should model cash timing as well as accounting margin. Payroll and infrastructure are paid before some invoices arrive, so a profitable contract can still create a liquidity crisis.']],
   ['A practical model review',['Begin with one representative customer. Write the acquisition path, expected activation date, support burden, payment schedule, variable infrastructure cost, retention assumption, and reason for expansion. Then test the most fragile number.','The goal is not to predict the future perfectly. It is to identify which behavior must be true, which evidence already exists, and which experiment can reduce uncertainty before scale magnifies the mistake.']]
  ],questions:['Which participant receives value and which participant pays?','What behavior makes retention likely?','Which cost grows each time usage grows?','How long must the company finance acquisition before recovering cash?'],terms:[['business model','a system for creating, delivering, and capturing value'],['contribution margin','revenue remaining after variable costs'],['customer acquisition cost','the cost of gaining a customer'],['payback period','the time required to recover acquisition spending']]},
  {code:'S23',title:'Venture Capital, Ownership, and Fundraising',summary:'The logic of venture returns, dilution, term sheets, and the decision to raise—or not raise—capital.',objective:'Evaluate fundraising as a strategic choice with consequences for ownership, governance, growth expectations, and risk.',sections:[
   ['Venture capital is designed for outliers',['A venture fund expects many investments to fail or return little and a small number to generate most of the portfolio’s value. This power-law structure pushes investors toward markets and companies capable of becoming unusually large.','A sound small business may therefore be a poor venture investment. The mismatch is not a judgment about quality; it is a difference between the company’s natural path and the return structure promised to a fund’s own investors.']],
   ['What a financing round changes',['Selling equity provides cash without scheduled repayment, but it changes ownership and governance. Founders trade part of the future company for time, talent, distribution, or speed today. Later rounds can dilute every earlier shareholder.','The relevant question is whether the capital increases the expected value of the remaining ownership enough to justify dilution and added obligations. More money is not automatically safer if it encourages premature hiring or creates expectations the market cannot support.']],
   ['Reading the term sheet',['Valuation receives attention, but control terms can matter as much. Board composition, liquidation preferences, participation rights, protective provisions, vesting, option pools, and pro rata rights influence outcomes during success, stagnation, and sale.','A high headline valuation combined with aggressive preferences can leave founders and employees with less than expected. Legal advice is necessary, but leaders must still understand the economic logic of what they sign.']],
   ['Fundraising as a process',['A credible round connects a clear market thesis, evidence of demand, a differentiated product, a capable team, and milestones the new capital can achieve. Data rooms and financial models should make uncertainty legible rather than bury it.','Running a process with a defined timeline can create focus and comparable choices. Endless fundraising distracts from customers and weakens leverage because the company appears unable to reach a decision.']],
   ['Alternatives and capital discipline',['Bootstrapping, revenue financing, grants, debt, strategic partnerships, and customer prepayments preserve different degrees of ownership and flexibility. Each source also introduces its own constraints.','The best financing strategy matches the business model. A capital-intensive biotechnology company and a profitable consulting product should not imitate the same playbook.']]
  ],questions:['Does this company have venture-scale return potential?','Which milestone becomes possible only with new capital?','Which term matters most in a downside outcome?','What source of funding best matches the company’s cash cycle?'],terms:[['dilution','reduction of an owner’s percentage after new shares are issued'],['term sheet','a summary of proposed investment terms'],['liquidation preference','the order and amount investors receive in an exit'],['power law','a distribution in which a few outcomes dominate returns']]},
  {code:'S24',title:'Culture, Hiring, and Operating Systems',summary:'How teams turn values into decisions, information flow, accountability, and repeatable execution.',objective:'Design an operating culture that supports learning and performance as a startup grows.',sections:[
   ['Culture is repeated behavior',['Culture is not a list of attractive nouns. It is the pattern of behavior that leaders reward, tolerate, and model when deadlines, revenue, or status are at risk. A company that praises candor but punishes disagreement teaches silence.','Values become useful when they describe a choice between two legitimate goods. “Move quickly by making reversible decisions close to the work” provides more guidance than “excellence.”']],
   ['Hiring under uncertainty',['Early hires shape product, norms, and future recruiting. A structured process defines outcomes for the role, tests job-relevant skills, uses consistent questions, and separates evidence from charisma.','Startups often overvalue similarity because it feels like speed. Diverse experience can improve problem solving, but only when the organization creates psychological safety and gives people real influence rather than symbolic representation.']],
   ['Goals and operating cadence',['An operating system connects strategy to weekly work. A small set of goals, clear owners, review meetings, written decisions, and accessible metrics reduce the need for constant executive intervention.','Metrics should support judgment, not replace it. Teams can manipulate targets or ignore unmeasured quality when incentives are narrow. Reviews need both numbers and narrative explanation.']],
   ['Conflict and accountability',['Healthy conflict attacks a problem without humiliating a person. Leaders should make the decision rule explicit: consultation, consent, individual ownership, or executive call. Ambiguity about who decides often creates more tension than disagreement itself.','Accountability means naming an expectation, checking evidence, and responding consistently. It does not mean searching for a person to blame whenever a system produces a predictable failure.']],
   ['Culture through transition',['Practices that worked for ten people may fail at one hundred. Informal context must become documented, managers need training, and communication paths must be redesigned without turning every action into bureaucracy.','The organization should preserve principles while changing mechanisms. A living culture can become more explicit without becoming less human.']]
  ],questions:['Which behavior does the company reward under pressure?','What evidence should a hiring decision require?','Which decisions need one accountable owner?','What must become explicit at the next stage of growth?'],terms:[['operating cadence','a repeated rhythm for planning and review'],['psychological safety','the ability to speak without fear of humiliation'],['decision rule','the method used to determine who decides'],['accountability','clear responsibility linked to evidence and follow-through']]}
 ],
 economy:[
  {code:'E07',title:'Behavioral Economics and Decision-Making',summary:'How attention, framing, heuristics, and institutions shape economic choice.',objective:'Use behavioral evidence to improve economic explanation without assuming that every deviation from a model is irrational.',sections:[
   ['The limits of perfect calculation',['Traditional models often describe people as consistent optimizers with stable preferences and complete use of available information. This abstraction can reveal important incentives, but actual decisions occur under time pressure, uncertainty, emotion, habit, and limited attention.','Behavioral economics studies systematic patterns in these conditions. The goal is not to prove that humans are foolish. It is to build models that better predict how real people choose and how environments shape those choices.']],
   ['Heuristics and bias',['Heuristics are shortcuts that make judgment possible. Availability leads vivid or recent events to feel more probable. Anchoring makes an initial number influence later estimates. Loss aversion describes the tendency for losses to weigh more heavily than comparable gains.','A shortcut can be useful in one environment and misleading in another. Calling every error a bias hides the question of what information, experience, or constraint made the response reasonable.']],
   ['Framing and defaults',['Equivalent choices can produce different behavior when described as gains or losses. Defaults are especially powerful because they reduce effort and may signal what an institution recommends. Enrollment in retirement savings, organ donation, and privacy settings all change when default rules change.','This power creates ethical obligations. A choice architecture should be transparent, easy to leave, and aligned with the person’s interests. A dark pattern uses the same knowledge to obstruct informed choice.']],
   ['Present bias and scarcity',['People often give disproportionate weight to immediate costs and benefits. This can undermine saving, preventive health, or long-term projects even when a person sincerely values them. Commitment devices attempt to protect a future plan from a predictable short-term temptation.','Scarcity also consumes attention. Financial insecurity can make urgent problems crowd out planning, producing decisions that look shortsighted while reflecting severe constraints. Better policy may need to reduce cognitive burden, not merely provide information.']],
   ['Behavior and institutions',['Individual behavior is shaped by wages, law, product design, social norms, and access to alternatives. A behavioral explanation should not replace structural analysis. Reminding workers to save cannot compensate for income that never covers basic needs.','The strongest approach asks how psychology and institutions interact. It tests interventions and measures whether benefits persist, who receives them, and whether autonomy is protected.']]
  ],questions:['When is a heuristic adaptive rather than biased?','Which defaults in your daily life influence you?','What separates a helpful nudge from manipulation?','How does scarcity change the meaning of an economic choice?'],terms:[['heuristic','a mental shortcut used for judgment'],['loss aversion','greater sensitivity to losses than equal gains'],['default','the option selected when no active choice is made'],['present bias','overweighting immediate outcomes relative to future ones']]},
  {code:'E08',title:'Labor, Wages, and the Changing Workplace',summary:'How productivity, institutions, bargaining power, technology, and care shape work and pay.',objective:'Explain wages and employment through both market forces and institutions.',sections:[
   ['Labor is not an ordinary commodity',['Work is sold through contracts, but it cannot be separated from the person who performs it. Hours, safety, dignity, schedule control, learning, and voice matter alongside pay. Workers also differ in their ability to wait, move, search, or refuse an offer.','A labor market therefore contains power relationships. Employers may have local dominance, workers may organize collectively, and law defines minimum standards and bargaining rights.']],
   ['Productivity and wages',['Productivity growth can support higher wages when workers produce more value per hour. Yet the distribution of those gains depends on ownership, competition, bargaining institutions, taxes, and corporate strategy. Higher productivity does not mechanically reach every worker.','Human capital—skills, education, experience, and health—matters, but access to it is unequal. Networks, discrimination, family resources, and geography affect who obtains opportunities and whose ability is recognized.']],
   ['Minimum wages and collective bargaining',['A minimum wage raises the legal floor. Its employment effects depend on the level, enforcement, local market power, demand, and firms’ ability to adjust prices, productivity, hours, or profits. Evidence should replace slogans on both sides.','Unions can raise wages, improve conditions, and give workers a collective voice. They can also face problems of representation and adaptation. Their influence varies with labor law and the structure of industries.']],
   ['Automation and task change',['Technology usually changes bundles of tasks rather than eliminating an occupation all at once. Some tasks are automated, others become more valuable, and new work appears. The transition can still be severe when regions or workers cannot move easily into new roles.','Policy choices affect whether productivity gains support broad prosperity. Training alone is insufficient without demand, income support, mobility, competition, and institutions that share the gains.']],
   ['Care and invisible work',['Households produce care, food, health, education, and emotional support, much of it unpaid and disproportionately performed by women. Conventional economic measures often exclude this work even though the formal economy depends on it.','Childcare, elder care, paid leave, and working time are therefore central economic institutions. They shape labor-force participation, well-being, and the distribution of opportunity across generations.']]
  ],questions:['Why can two equally productive workers receive different wages?','When does an employer possess market power?','Which tasks in a job are most likely to change through automation?','How should economies value unpaid care?'],terms:[['labor market','the institutions and exchanges through which work is organized'],['bargaining power','the capacity to influence terms of an agreement'],['human capital','skills, knowledge, experience, and health'],['care economy','paid and unpaid work that sustains people']]},
  {code:'E09',title:'Climate Economics and the Energy Transition',summary:'Externalities, carbon pricing, innovation, adaptation, and the distributional politics of climate policy.',objective:'Compare economic tools for reducing emissions while accounting for uncertainty, justice, and political feasibility.',sections:[
   ['Climate change as an economic problem',['Greenhouse-gas emissions create costs that are not fully included in market prices. Because the atmosphere is shared and the effects cross borders and generations, individual transactions can produce global externalities.','The problem contains deep uncertainty and irreversible risks. Waiting for perfect knowledge is itself a choice because infrastructure built today can lock in emissions for decades.']],
   ['Carbon prices and regulation',['A carbon tax or emissions market creates a price for pollution and lets firms search for lower-cost reductions. The result depends on coverage, enforcement, expectations, and how revenue is used.','Standards, public investment, and direct regulation can be more effective when consumers lack information, infrastructure is missing, or innovation requires coordinated change. Most serious strategies combine instruments rather than rely on one.']],
   ['Innovation and systems',['Solar panels, batteries, grids, buildings, transport, industry, and land use form connected systems. Learning by doing can reduce costs as deployment grows, while network infrastructure can make clean alternatives practical.','Public research, procurement, and early subsidies can accelerate technologies whose social value exceeds the return any one firm can capture. Policy should also avoid locking support into a permanently uncompetitive design.']],
   ['Adaptation and resilience',['Even rapid mitigation cannot eliminate all damage. Adaptation includes heat planning, water systems, resilient agriculture, coastal protection, insurance reform, and public-health capacity.','Poorer communities often face greater exposure and fewer resources to adapt. Cost-benefit analysis must not treat a dollar of loss as equally significant to households with radically different wealth and security.']],
   ['A just transition',['Workers and regions tied to fossil-fuel industries may bear concentrated costs while climate benefits are widely shared. Income support, retraining, regional investment, pension protection, and participation in planning can make transition both fairer and more durable.','Climate policy is therefore not only about selecting efficient technology. It is a conflict over time, risk, responsibility, and who has the power to design the future.']]
  ],questions:['What makes climate change a global externality?','When is pricing preferable to direct regulation?','Which infrastructure creates clean alternatives rather than merely discouraging dirty ones?','Who should bear the costs of transition and why?'],terms:[['carbon price','a monetary cost assigned to greenhouse-gas emissions'],['mitigation','action that reduces emissions or increases carbon removal'],['adaptation','adjustment to actual or expected climate effects'],['just transition','a shift that protects affected workers and communities']]}
 ],
 cinema:[
  {code:'C09',title:'Documentary: Evidence, Voice, and Ethics',summary:'How nonfiction cinema constructs reality through selection, form, testimony, and responsibility.',objective:'Analyze documentary as an authored representation rather than a transparent window on reality.',sections:[
   ['Reality must still be constructed',['A documentary records people, places, and events, but every film selects a frame, duration, sequence, and point of view. The presence of real-world referents does not remove authorship. It increases the need to ask how evidence was gathered and arranged.','A camera can observe, provoke, collaborate, conceal, or perform. The meaning of footage depends on sound, editing, titles, narration, and the circumstances of production.']],
   ['Documentary modes',['Expository documentaries organize evidence around an argument, often through narration. Observational works minimize visible intervention. Participatory films include the filmmaker’s encounters, while reflexive films reveal the process and limits of representation.','Poetic and performative approaches may privilege association, memory, embodiment, or subjective truth. These modes often overlap within one film.']],
   ['Archive and testimony',['Archival images seem to bring the past directly into the present, yet they were created for particular purposes and may lack context. Reframing them can expose suppressed histories or produce a new distortion.','Testimony provides lived knowledge but is not raw data. Memory is shaped by trauma, time, language, and the relationship between speaker, interviewer, and audience. Ethical editing preserves complexity rather than extracting only the most dramatic sentence.']],
   ['Consent and power',['Filmmakers control equipment, access, financing, and the final cut. A participant may agree to be filmed without understanding future circulation or the consequences of exposure. Legal consent is therefore only a minimum.','Responsible practice considers ongoing consent, safety, compensation, translation, community relationships, and whether a film turns another person’s suffering into prestige for its maker.']],
   ['Truth beyond neutrality',['Documentary truth does not require pretending to have no perspective. It requires honesty about methods, strong evidence, fair treatment of uncertainty, and forms that allow viewers to understand how claims were produced.','A film can advocate a position and remain rigorous. Neutral appearance can be misleading when it gives false equivalence to claims with radically different evidence.']]
  ],questions:['What does the camera’s presence change?','Who controls the context of archival footage?','Can consent remain valid when a film reaches an unexpected audience?','How can advocacy preserve factual rigor?'],terms:[['documentary mode','a recurring strategy for representing reality'],['testimony','a personal account that bears witness'],['reflexivity','attention to the process of representation itself'],['informed consent','agreement based on meaningful understanding of risks and use']]},
  {code:'C10',title:'Animation: Drawing Movement and Building Worlds',summary:'From hand-drawn frames and stop motion to anime, digital production, and experimental form.',objective:'Understand animation as a diverse cinematic language rather than a genre intended only for children.',sections:[
   ['Movement created frame by frame',['Live-action cinema records movement before a camera; animation constructs or transforms movement between frames. Drawings, objects, puppets, paint, cut paper, sand, models, and digital images can all become cinematic through controlled change over time.','This freedom makes animation especially suited to metamorphosis. A body can stretch, a room can breathe, and a symbol can become a landscape without obeying physical continuity.']],
   ['Industrial systems and style',['Studio animation divided labor among story, layout, key poses, in-between frames, ink, paint, camera, sound, and editing. Limited animation reduced the number of drawings and turned constraint into a distinct graphic rhythm.','Technologies never determine style alone. The same digital tool can imitate photographic realism, preserve visible brushwork, or produce radical abstraction depending on artistic and economic choices.']],
   ['Anime and visual economy',['Japanese animation developed varied traditions across cinema and television. Selective movement, strong composition, detailed environments, and shifts between stillness and action can concentrate attention differently from continuous full animation.','Anime is not one style or genre. It includes children’s stories, historical drama, science fiction, romance, horror, documentary experiment, and adult social criticism.']],
   ['Stop motion and material presence',['Stop-motion animation photographs physical objects after small adjustments. The viewer perceives both imagined life and the texture of materials: fingerprints, fabric, clay, wood, light, and gravity.','Its labor-intensive process creates a special relation between time and craft. Imperfections can make the animated world feel more tangible rather than less convincing.']],
   ['Animation as thought',['Animation can visualize memory, data, dreams, trauma, and events that were never filmed. It can simplify reality into diagrams or multiply it into impossible perspectives.','The key analytical question is not whether the image is realistic, but which rules of movement, space, and transformation the film creates—and what those rules allow the film to think.']]
  ],questions:['How does constructed movement differ from recorded movement?','What aesthetic possibilities emerge from limited resources?','Why is animation not a single genre?','What can an animated image represent that live action cannot?'],terms:[['key frame','a principal drawing or pose that defines movement'],['in-between','a frame created between key poses'],['stop motion','animation made by photographing adjusted physical objects'],['metamorphosis','visible transformation from one form into another']]},
  {code:'C11',title:'Latin American Cinema: Modernity, Memory, and Resistance',summary:'Political filmmaking, popular genres, dictatorship, migration, and new transnational voices.',objective:'Trace major debates in Latin American cinema without reducing the region to one style or political program.',sections:[
   ['Industries and national audiences',['Mexico, Argentina, and Brazil developed major studio cultures, stars, music, melodrama, comedy, and popular genres. Cinema helped imagine national identities while also circulating across borders.','Industrial strength varied with state policy, market size, distribution, and competition from Hollywood. National cinema was never isolated from international technology or finance.']],
   ['Third Cinema and political form',['During the 1960s and 1970s, filmmakers associated with Third Cinema criticized both Hollywood entertainment and isolated art cinema. They imagined film as a collective political practice connected to anti-imperial and revolutionary movements.','Documentary, essay, clandestine exhibition, montage, and direct address challenged passive spectatorship. The movement’s manifestos remain influential, although their language sometimes simplified audiences and internal differences.']],
   ['Cinema Novo and imperfect cinema',['Brazilian Cinema Novo confronted hunger, land, class, race, and authoritarian modernization through formally inventive work. Cuban debates over imperfect cinema valued urgent participation over polished dependence on expensive industrial standards.','Scarcity became an aesthetic and political question: should filmmakers imitate dominant production values, or invent forms adequate to their own conditions?']],
   ['Dictatorship, exile, and memory',['Military dictatorships censored artists, destroyed institutions, and forced filmmakers into exile. Later films returned to disappearance, testimony, complicity, and intergenerational memory.','Memory cinema does more than reconstruct facts. It asks who may speak, how absence can be represented, and why democratic societies continue to struggle over the meaning of past violence.']],
   ['Contemporary transnational cinema',['Recent filmmakers work through co-productions, festivals, streaming platforms, regional funds, and diasporic networks. Their films address migration, Indigenous sovereignty, gender, urban inequality, ecology, and the afterlives of colonialism.','Transnational support can expand production while also shaping which stories become legible to global markets. Strong analysis follows both the image on screen and the institutions that made its circulation possible.']]
  ],questions:['How did popular genres participate in national identity?','What made Third Cinema a practice rather than only a style?','How can film represent disappearance without turning it into spectacle?','What opportunities and pressures come with transnational funding?'],terms:[['Third Cinema','political filmmaking opposed to colonial and commercial domination'],['Cinema Novo','a Brazilian movement linking formal invention and social critique'],['exile cinema','film shaped by forced displacement'],['co-production','a film financed or produced across multiple partners or countries']]}
 ],
 literature:[
  {code:'L12',title:'Poetry: Voice, Image, Rhythm, and Form',summary:'How poems organize sound, line, metaphor, and silence to create concentrated meaning.',objective:'Develop a method for reading poetry through formal evidence rather than paraphrase alone.',sections:[
   ['A poem is an event in language',['A poem may tell a story or state an idea, but its meaning also occurs through sound, pace, spacing, repetition, and the tension between sentence and line. Paraphrase can identify a topic while missing the experience the poem constructs.','Close reading therefore begins with description. Who speaks? To whom? Where do syntax and line breaks cooperate or resist each other? Which words return, and which expectations are delayed?']],
   ['Speaker and situation',['The speaker of a poem is not automatically the author. A dramatic monologue may present a voice whose judgments the poem invites us to question. Even an apparently personal lyric selects, arranges, and stylizes experience.','Identifying the situation prevents vague interpretation. A declaration spoken privately, publicly, retrospectively, or under threat carries different stakes.']],
   ['Image and figurative language',['An image addresses perception; a metaphor reorganizes understanding by bringing unlike fields into relation. Its force depends on both similarity and difference. A successful metaphor does not simply decorate a preexisting idea—it can make a new thought possible.','Symbols and motifs develop through recurrence. Their meanings remain connected to context and change as the poem progresses.']],
   ['Rhythm, meter, and sound',['Meter creates an expected pattern of stress, while rhythm includes the actual movement of speech. Variation matters because it can emphasize hesitation, speed, rupture, or return. Rhyme links distant words and may produce harmony, irony, or constraint.','Sound devices such as alliteration, assonance, and consonance create relationships that are partly semantic and partly physical. Reading aloud reveals structures the eye may miss.']],
   ['Line, stanza, and silence',['A line break can complete a unit, suspend it, or force a word to belong momentarily to two constructions. Stanzas organize development like paragraphs, scenes, or musical phrases.','White space and silence are not empty. They can mark loss, censorship, breath, distance, or the limits of speech. Form becomes interpretation when each choice is connected to the poem’s central pressure.']]
  ],questions:['How does the speaker differ from the author?','What does a metaphor allow the poem to think?','Where does rhythm break an established expectation?','What meaning is created by a line break or silence?'],terms:[['lyric speaker','the voice that speaks within a poem'],['enjambment','syntax continuing beyond the end of a poetic line'],['meter','a recurring pattern of stressed and unstressed syllables'],['imagery','language that evokes sensory experience']]},
  {code:'L13',title:'Narrative Theory: Time, Voice, and Point of View',summary:'The tools through which stories control information, sequence, perspective, and reliability.',objective:'Analyze narrative form by separating events from the way a narrator presents them.',sections:[
   ['Story and discourse',['The story is the chain of events we reconstruct; discourse is the order, duration, voice, and language through which those events are presented. A novel may reveal the final outcome first, repeat one scene from several perspectives, or omit the decisive event.','This distinction explains why two narratives can contain similar events and produce radically different meanings. Form controls what readers know, when they know it, and how certain that knowledge can be.']],
   ['Order, duration, and frequency',['Flashback and anticipation rearrange chronology. Summary compresses years, scene slows time toward dialogue, and pause suspends action for description or reflection. Ellipsis removes a span readers must infer.','A narrative can tell once what happened repeatedly, repeat one event many times, or describe habitual actions. These choices shape memory and emphasis.']],
   ['Narrator and reliability',['A first-person narrator has direct access to one consciousness but limited access to the world. A third-person narrator may move widely or remain closely aligned with one character. Grammatical person alone does not determine knowledge.','Unreliability emerges when a narrator’s account conflicts with evidence, values, or patterns the text allows readers to perceive. The effect can create irony, intimacy, suspense, or ethical discomfort.']],
   ['Focalization and perspective',['Focalization asks through whose perception information is filtered. A passage can be narrated in the third person while remaining saturated with a character’s vocabulary, judgments, and blind spots.','Shifts in focalization redistribute sympathy and authority. Multiperspectival narratives can expose contradictions without providing a neutral final view.']],
   ['Narrative and power',['The ability to tell a story includes the power to define beginnings, causes, protagonists, and endings. Colonial archives, family histories, legal testimony, and national myths all rely on narrative choices.','Literary analysis becomes political when it shows precisely whose perspective is centered, whose experience is summarized, and which events are treated as inevitable.']]
  ],questions:['What changes when events are presented out of order?','Which evidence makes a narrator unreliable?','Who perceives the scene, even when another voice narrates it?','Which character or group is denied narrative time?'],terms:[['discourse','the manner and order in which a story is presented'],['focalization','the perspective through which narrative information is filtered'],['unreliable narrator','a narrator whose account cannot be accepted without question'],['ellipsis','the omission of a period or event from narration']]},
  {code:'L14',title:'World Literature and the Work of Translation',summary:'How texts travel across languages, markets, empires, classrooms, and changing communities of readers.',objective:'Study translation as interpretation and world literature as a system of unequal circulation.',sections:[
   ['What makes literature worldly',['A text becomes part of world literature not only because it describes the world, but because it travels beyond its first context. Translation, migration, education, publishing, prizes, adaptation, and criticism create new audiences.','Circulation transforms meaning. A novel read as local satire at home may be marketed abroad as national testimony. Readers bring different histories, and publishers frame the work through covers, introductions, and categories.']],
   ['Translation as decision',['Languages divide experience differently. Translators choose among literal structure, rhythm, idiom, cultural explanation, ambiguity, and readability. No version transfers every feature without change.','This does not make translation a failure. It makes it a disciplined interpretation with visible priorities. Comparing versions can reveal possibilities that monolingual reading leaves unnoticed.']],
   ['Domestication and foreignness',['A domesticating translation makes a text feel familiar in the receiving language. A foreignizing strategy preserves some difference and reminds readers that they are encountering another linguistic world. Most translations combine both.','The ethical question is not simply which strategy is correct, but whose comfort is prioritized and which cultural distinctions disappear.']],
   ['Unequal circulation',['Languages with powerful publishing industries export more books and attract more translation funding. Works from less dominant languages may be selected only when they match expectations about trauma, exoticism, politics, or authenticity.','World literature is therefore not a neutral shelf containing the best of every culture. It is shaped by institutions, markets, geopolitical power, and the labor of translators who are often made invisible.']],
   ['Reading across contexts',['A responsible reader needs neither total expertise nor the illusion that context is irrelevant. Paratexts, historical research, reviews from the source culture, and attention to translation can correct easy assumptions.','The aim is not to freeze a work inside its origin. It is to let multiple contexts interact while remaining alert to asymmetries of knowledge and power.']]
  ],questions:['How does a book change when it enters a new market?','Which features of a text are hardest to translate?','When should a translation preserve difficulty?','Who decides which works represent a language or nation abroad?'],terms:[['source text','the text from which a translation is made'],['target language','the language into which a text is translated'],['domestication','translation that emphasizes familiarity for target readers'],['paratext','material such as titles, covers, notes, and introductions surrounding a work']]}
 ]
};

function render(entry,course){
 const sections=entry.sections.map(([heading,paragraphs])=>`<h2 class="source-heading">${esc(heading)}</h2>${paragraphs.map(text=>`<p class="source-paragraph">${esc(text)}</p>`).join('')}`).join('');
 const questions=entry.questions.map(text=>`<li>${esc(text)}</li>`).join('');
 const terms=entry.terms.map(([term,meaning])=>`<tr><td><strong>${esc(term)}</strong></td><td>${esc(meaning)}</td></tr>`).join('');
 return `<article class="source-page reading-article expanded-reading"><p class="source-kicker">${esc(courseNames[course])} · ${esc(entry.code)}</p><blockquote class="notion-quote"><p><strong>Objective:</strong> ${esc(entry.objective)}</p></blockquote>${sections}<h2 class="source-heading">Questions for study</h2><ol class="thematic-list">${questions}</ol><h2 class="source-heading">Key terms</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table class="source-table"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>${terms}</tbody></table></div></article>`;
}

const nextOrder={};
Object.keys(courseNames).forEach(course=>{nextOrder[course]=Math.max(0,...app.entries.filter(entry=>entry.theme===course).map(entry=>Number(entry.themeOrder)||0));});
Object.entries(newReadings).forEach(([course,entries])=>entries.forEach((entry,index)=>app.entries.push({
 id:`expanded-${course}-${entry.code.toLowerCase()}`,title:`${entry.code} · ${entry.title}`,summary:entry.summary,category:'reading',level:'B2',allowedLevels:['B1','B2','C1','C2'],page:0,endPage:0,depth:1,theme:course,themeOrder:nextOrder[course]+index+1,readingCourse:true,html:render(entry,course)
})));

const deeperLenses={
 philosophy:[
  ['Context and interpretation','Philosophical positions emerge from disputes rather than from isolated quotations. Read this chapter by identifying the problem that makes its concepts necessary, the argument offered in response, and the strongest objection the position must answer. A concept becomes clearer when we can state not only its definition but also what it allows a thinker to explain.'],
  ['From doctrine to argument','Avoid treating an author as a list of conclusions. Reconstruct the movement from question to premise to conclusion, then ask which historical assumptions support that movement. Productive comparison does not force thinkers to say the same thing; it locates the precise point where their reasons diverge.'],
  ['Philosophy here and now','The value of a philosophical text is tested when its concepts illuminate a present conflict without becoming a slogan. Apply the chapter cautiously: preserve differences between historical contexts, name what the concept reveals, and also identify what it leaves outside the frame.']
 ],
 history:[
  ['Evidence and scale','Historical explanation changes with scale. An event may appear as a leader’s decision at one level, a social conflict at another, and part of a long economic transformation at a third. Compare these levels and ask which sources make each account possible.'],
  ['Cause, contingency, and consequence','A historical outcome was not inevitable simply because it happened. Distinguish long-term conditions from immediate triggers, and separate the intentions of participants from consequences they could not foresee. Counterfactual questions can expose which causes were necessary and which were replaceable.'],
  ['Voices and archives','Archives preserve power unevenly. States and elites often leave abundant records, while workers, women, Indigenous communities, migrants, and enslaved people may appear through hostile or fragmentary sources. Read both what a document says and the institutional purpose that produced it.']
 ],
 startups:[
  ['From principle to operating decision','A startup concept matters only when it changes a decision. Translate the central idea of this reading into a hypothesis, the evidence that would support it, the metric that could mislead the team, and the condition that would require a different strategy.'],
  ['Systems rather than isolated tactics','Product, distribution, pricing, retention, finance, and organization interact. A local improvement can damage the whole system—for example, faster acquisition can overwhelm support or attract customers who never retain. Trace second-order effects before calling a result growth.'],
  ['The ethical and strategic boundary','Short-term performance can hide long-term damage to trust, autonomy, employees, or the market. Examine who benefits, who bears risk, which information is asymmetric, and whether the tactic would remain acceptable if users and workers fully understood it.']
 ],
 economy:[
  ['Models and their assumptions','An economic model simplifies reality to make a relationship visible. Its usefulness depends on the question, the assumptions held constant, and the evidence used to test it. Ask what the model explains well, which institutions it treats as fixed, and whose behavior does not fit its representative agent.'],
  ['Distribution behind the average','An average can improve while a large group becomes worse off. Track how costs, income, risk, time, and bargaining power are distributed across households, firms, regions, and generations. Efficiency and fairness are distinct questions, even when they influence one another.'],
  ['Policy as institutional design','Policies change incentives, but they also require administration, information, legitimacy, and enforcement. Compare the ideal mechanism with the institution that would implement it. A theoretically efficient tool can fail when compliance is weak or affected groups have no viable alternative.']
 ],
 cinema:[
  ['Form as evidence','Begin by describing what the film does before deciding what it means. Shot duration, framing, movement, performance, sound, color, and editing direct attention and create relationships. An interpretation becomes persuasive when it connects a formal pattern to a historical or emotional effect.'],
  ['Industry and authorship','Films are made through collective labor inside systems of finance, technology, censorship, distribution, and exhibition. The director may be important, but production design, acting, cinematography, editing, and sound also shape the work. Link aesthetic choices to the conditions that made them possible.'],
  ['Spectatorship and power','A film positions viewers: it grants knowledge, withholds context, creates identification, and defines which bodies can be seen as complex. Ask who looks, who is looked at, whose perspective organizes the scene, and how genre expectations influence judgment.']
 ],
 literature:[
  ['Close reading and historical context','Close reading and contextual study strengthen one another. Syntax, image, rhythm, narration, and genre show how a text thinks, while historical research explains which conventions and conflicts those choices transform. Move between a precise passage and the larger structure.'],
  ['Form is part of the argument','Literature does not simply place an idea inside decorative language. Voice, order, repetition, silence, perspective, and genre determine what can be known and felt. Ask why this meaning needed this form rather than a neutral summary.'],
  ['Reading against the obvious','The first interpretation is often the one a culture has made familiar. Test it by following contradictions, minor characters, shifts in tone, and moments the narrator cannot control. A strong reading accounts for resistant details instead of discarding them.']
 ]
};

const workshops={
 philosophy:{title:'Argument workshop',paragraphs:[
  'Write the chapter’s main argument as a sequence of claims rather than as a list of names. Begin with the question, state the conclusion, and identify the reasons that are supposed to support it. Then define every concept that carries argumentative weight. Words such as freedom, nature, reason, justice, and truth often seem familiar while hiding several incompatible meanings.',
  'Construct the strongest objection you can. An objection should target a premise, an inference, an example, or a consequence; simple disagreement is not enough. Next, imagine how the author might reply. This exchange reveals whether the position can absorb criticism, needs revision, or depends on a background commitment that the text never fully defends.',
  'Finish with a transfer test. Choose a contemporary case and apply the concept carefully. Record what becomes newly visible, what remains unexplained, and which differences between the original context and the present limit the comparison. This prevents philosophical vocabulary from becoming decoration.'
 ],steps:['State the question and conclusion in one sentence each.','Define the two concepts that do the most work.','Develop one serious objection and one possible reply.','Apply the revised argument to a concrete case.']},
 history:{title:'Historical investigation',paragraphs:[
  'Build a short chronology containing conditions, turning points, and consequences. Chronology alone is not explanation, but it prevents causes from being assigned to forces that appeared only after the outcome. Mark what historical actors knew at each moment; later knowledge should not be projected backward into their decisions.',
  'Create a source table with author, date, audience, purpose, and silence. A law may reveal an official objective without proving that the rule was enforced. A testimony can preserve experience while remaining shaped by memory and circumstance. Material evidence can contradict both. Reliable interpretation emerges by placing unlike sources in conversation.',
  'Compare at least two explanations: political, economic, social, environmental, cultural, or technological. Decide which evidence each explains well and where it fails. A developed historical account usually combines levels without turning one factor into a universal cause.'
 ],steps:['Place the main events in chronological order.','Separate long-term conditions from immediate triggers.','Evaluate one source for purpose and limitation.','Compare two explanations of the outcome.']},
 startups:{title:'Application workshop',paragraphs:[
  'Turn the reading into a decision memo. Name the customer or stakeholder, the problem, the current alternative, and the behavior that would count as evidence. A useful memo separates facts already observed from assumptions, estimates, and preferences. It also gives each major uncertainty an owner and a date for review.',
  'Design the smallest test that could change the decision. Specify the population, intervention, primary metric, time window, and threshold. Add a guardrail metric for possible harm such as complaints, churn, support load, cash burn, or loss of trust. Before launching, write what the team will do under positive, negative, and ambiguous results.',
  'Finally, examine system effects. Ask what breaks if the result is ten times larger, which cost grows faster than revenue, and which manual process is hiding inside the current experience. Growth is credible only when value, operations, economics, and responsibility can develop together.'
 ],steps:['Separate evidence from assumptions.','Choose one decision-changing experiment.','Define a primary metric and a guardrail.','Model what happens at ten times the current scale.']},
 economy:{title:'Evidence and policy workshop',paragraphs:[
  'Translate the chapter into a simple causal diagram. Identify the decision-makers, constraints, incentives, information, and institutions that connect an intervention to an outcome. Mark possible feedback loops and variables that influence both the proposed cause and the result. This reveals why correlation alone may be misleading.',
  'Disaggregate the expected outcome. Estimate who gains, who pays, who carries risk, and when each effect arrives. A policy can raise total output while increasing insecurity for a particular region or generation. Distribution should be analyzed directly rather than postponed until after efficiency has been calculated.',
  'Compare at least two policy instruments and the option of doing nothing. Include administrative capacity, enforcement, political response, unintended adaptation, and the possibility of learning through a pilot. Good economic analysis makes assumptions visible and treats uncertainty as information to manage, not as a reason to avoid judgment.'
 ],steps:['Draw the causal chain from action to outcome.','Identify one confounder or feedback loop.','Disaggregate benefits, costs, and risk.','Compare two instruments and their implementation demands.']},
 cinema:{title:'Scene analysis workshop',paragraphs:[
  'Select one sequence and watch it first without stopping, then again shot by shot. Record duration, framing, camera position, movement, light, color, performance, sound, and transitions. Description should come before interpretation so that the argument remains answerable to what is actually on screen.',
  'Map how information is distributed. Note what the viewer knows, what a character knows, and what remains outside the frame. Identify the moment when attention, sympathy, or expectation changes. Editing and sound often produce this shift before dialogue names it.',
  'Connect the sequence to a larger context: genre convention, production system, technology, censorship, star image, historical conflict, or audience. The final interpretation should explain why these formal choices matter and consider one plausible alternative reading rather than presenting symbolism as a secret code.'
 ],steps:['Describe the sequence shot by shot.','Track changes in knowledge and attention.','Connect one formal pattern to an effect.','Test the interpretation against an alternative reading.']},
 literature:{title:'Close-reading workshop',paragraphs:[
  'Choose a passage of five to fifteen lines and mark repetitions, contrasts, images, pronouns, tense changes, punctuation, and unusual syntax. Read it aloud. Sound and rhythm may create emphasis that a paraphrase misses. Identify where the passage changes direction and which word carries the greatest pressure.',
  'Move outward from the passage. Ask how narrator, genre, structure, and historical context alter its meaning. A metaphor can echo an earlier scene; a minor grammatical shift can change responsibility; a convention can be repeated sincerely or transformed ironically. Quote only the smallest evidence needed, then explain how its form produces the claimed effect.',
  'Test the reading against a resistant detail. If one image, scene, or voice does not fit, revise the interpretation instead of ignoring it. Developed literary analysis does not discover a hidden message once and stop; it builds an account capable of explaining both the dominant pattern and its tensions.'
 ],steps:['Annotate the passage’s formal patterns.','Locate its turning point.','Connect the passage to the whole work and context.','Revise the claim to include a resistant detail.']}
};

app.entries.filter(entry=>courseNames[entry.theme]).forEach((entry,index)=>{
 if(entry.html.includes('class="deeper-reading"'))return;
 const [heading,text]=deeperLenses[entry.theme][index%deeperLenses[entry.theme].length];
 const workshop=workshops[entry.theme];
 const prompts=[`What central problem organizes “${entry.title.replace(/^[A-Z]\d+\s*·\s*/,'')}”?`,'Which passage or example provides the strongest evidence?','Which assumption could a critic challenge?','How does the reading change your interpretation of a present-day issue?'];
 const extension=`<section class="deeper-reading"><h2 class="source-heading">${esc(heading)}</h2><p class="source-paragraph">${esc(text)}</p><p class="source-paragraph">Return to the chapter’s central claim—${esc(entry.summary||entry.title)}—and test it through comparison, evidence, and counterexample. The aim is to move from recognition to explanation: state the claim in your own words, show how it works, and define the limit beyond which it should not be applied.</p><h2 class="source-heading">${esc(workshop.title)}</h2>${workshop.paragraphs.map(paragraph=>`<p class="source-paragraph">${esc(paragraph)}</p>`).join('')}<ol class="thematic-list">${workshop.steps.map(step=>`<li>${esc(step)}</li>`).join('')}</ol><h2 class="source-heading">Study prompts</h2><ul class="thematic-list">${prompts.map(prompt=>`<li>${esc(prompt)}</li>`).join('')}</ul></section>`;
 entry.html=entry.html.replace(/<\/article>\s*$/,`${extension}</article>`);
});

Object.assign(app.themes.philosophy,{description:'Arguments, authors, and problems from classical philosophy to ethics, political thought, mind, language, and Latin American debates.'});
Object.assign(app.themes.history,{description:'A connected chronology of world and Peruvian history built through evidence, comparison, conflict, and historical interpretation.'});
Object.assign(app.themes.startups,{description:'Product, markets, growth, finance, culture, and leadership—from early validation to the operating systems required at scale.'});
Object.assign(app.themes.economy,{description:'Microeconomics, macroeconomics, behavior, labor, inequality, trade, development, and the economics of climate transition.'});
Object.assign(app.themes.cinema,{description:'Film history and visual language across fiction, documentary, animation, world cinema, and Latin American traditions.'});
Object.assign(app.themes.literature,{description:'Authors, movements, genres, poetry, narrative theory, translation, and literary form from Classical Antiquity to the present.'});
})();
